.Dd September 16, 2026
.Dt SUNNDI 9F
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm sunndi ,
.Nm sunndi.h
.Nd nexus driver interfaces
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/sunndi.h
.Sh INTERFACE LEVEL
illumos specific
.Pq Consolidation Private .
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The \fBsys/sunndi.h\fP header declares nexus driver interfaces used by bus nexus
drivers and the illumos device framework.
Its declarations are available when building kernel code with \fB_KERNEL\fP
defined.
These interfaces are specific to illumos and include internal framework
operations; inclusion in this header does not make an interface part of the
stable, portable DDI.
.Pp
A nexus describes devices below it, supplies bus operations through its
\fBdev_ops\fP structure, and coordinates their configuration and removal.
These interfaces provide the device-tree, property, event, hotplug, and
resource services needed for that work.
For ordinary leaf-driver operations, consult \fBIntro(9F)\fP and the DDI pages.
.Ss Device nodes and locking
Device nodes progress from allocation through linking, driver binding,
parent initialization, and driver attachment.
An allocated prototype is not yet an attached device.
Persistent nodes represent firmware or software enumeration of hardware;
nonpersistent configuration-file nodes can be discarded when attachment
fails.
Persistence is a device-tree lifetime attribute, not storage across reboots.
.Pp
Keep every node valid while using it.
\fBndi_hold_devi()\fP protects a node's lifetime once the hold has safely been
acquired.
\fBndi_devi_enter()\fP serializes changes to its children and does not replace
that lifetime protection.
Acquire critical sections from ancestors towards descendants.
Functions that return child pointers differ in whether they return a held
reference; follow each function's ownership rules.
.Ss Function groups
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Xr ndi_prop_update 9F
Create and update hardware properties on enumerated nodes.
.It Xr ndi_prop_remove 9F
Remove hardware properties.
.It Xr ndi_devi_alloc 9F
Allocate, name, and free prototype device nodes.
.It Xr ndi_devi_enter 9F
Serialize changes to a nexus's children.
.It Xr ndi_hold_devi 9F
Maintain device-node references.
.It Xr ndi_hold_driver 9F
Maintain driver-module references.
.It Xr ndi_devi_online 9F
Bind, attach, offline, and asynchronously configure a node.
.It Xr ndi_devi_config 9F
Configure and unconfigure children and descendants.
.It Xr ndi_devi_findchild 9F
Find immediate children by name, binding, and address.
.It Xr ndi_devi_device_remove 9F
Record physical removal and reinsertion.
.It Xr ndi_dev_is_prom_node 9F
Inspect node origin, persistence, and visibility.
.It Xr ndi_busop_bus_config 9F
Implement default nexus bus configuration operations.
.It Xr ndi_devi_config_vhci 9F
Create framework virtual HCI nodes.
.It Xr ndi_merge_node 9F
Merge driver configuration-file properties.
.It Xr ndi_set_bus_private 9F
Store bus-private pointers and port tags.
.It Xr ndi_flavorv_alloc 9F
Associate private pointers with child flavors.
.It Xr ndi_devctl_ioctl 9F
Handle common nexus device-control requests.
.It Xr ndi_dc_allochdl 9F
Copy in and access device-control request data.
.It Xr ndi_dc_return_dev_state 9F
Copy state results to a requesting process.
.It Xr ndi_dc_devi_create 9F
Create a child from a device-control property list.
.It Xr ndi_get_bus_state 9F
Read and record bus software state.
.It Xr ndi_event_alloc_hdl 9F
Allocate event handles and bind definitions.
.It Xr ndi_event_retrieve_cookie 9F
Look up event cookies and translate tags and names.
.It Xr ndi_event_add_callback 9F
Register callbacks and deliver events.
.It Xr ndi_post_event 9F
Dispatch event operations to the responsible nexus.
.It Xr ndi_event_dump_hdl 9F
Inspect event-framework state in debug builds.
.It Xr ndi_irm_create 9F
Manage interrupt resource pools.
.It Xr ndi_hp_register 9F
Register connections and request hotplug state changes.
.It Xr ndi_ra_alloc 9F
Manage bus resource maps and allocate ranges.
.It Xr ndi_set_acc_fault 9F
Set and clear nexus access/DMA fault indications.
.El
.Ss Structures
\fBndi_event_definition(9S)\fP and \fBndi_event_set(9S)\fP describe the events a
nexus supplies.
\fBndi_event_cookie(9S)\fP and \fBndi_event_callbacks(9S)\fP describe internal
event bookkeeping that drivers must access through the event interfaces.
\fBndi_ra_request(9S)\fP describes bus resource allocation constraints.
\fBddi_fault_event_data(9S)\fP describes the device-fault event payload.
.Ss Status and flags
Many functions return \fBNDI_SUCCESS\fP or an NDI failure status, but property
functions use \fBDDI_PROP_*\fP values and some device-control functions use
errno values or a mixture of conventions.
Predicate functions return boolean results.
There is no kernel \fBerrno\fP variable.
Always use the return convention documented for the specific function.
.Pp
The general NDI status definitions are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Dv NDI_SUCCESS
Operation succeeded; equal to \fBDDI_SUCCESS\fP.
.It Dv NDI_FAILURE
Operation failed; equal to \fBDDI_FAILURE\fP.
.It Dv NDI_NOMEM
Resources could not be allocated.
.It Dv NDI_BADHANDLE
Invalid handle status; not every handle-taking function validates it.
.It Dv NDI_FAULT
Failure while copying request data.
.It Dv NDI_BUSY
A busy device or resource prevents the operation.
.It Dv NDI_UNBOUND
Device is not bound to a driver.
.It Dv NDI_EINVAL
Invalid request or arguments.
.It Dv NDI_ENOTSUP
Unsupported operation or event.
.It Dv NDI_CLAIMED
Request claimed; equal to \fBNDI_SUCCESS\fP.
.It Dv NDI_UNCLAIMED
Request not claimed.
.El
.Pp
Not all functions return every status listed above.
The resource allocator also defines \fBNDI_RA_PARTIAL_REQ\fP, which indicates
an allocation that must be accounted for even though it is smaller than
requested.
.Pp
\fBNDI_SLEEP\fP and \fBNDI_NOSLEEP\fP select allocation behavior only for functions
that document accepting them.
\fBNDI_SLEEP\fP is zero.
They do not change a function's locking or execution-context requirements.
Property updates always use non-sleeping allocations.
.Pp
Configuration and event flags apply to their documented operation families;
the header's internal framework flags are not universally interchangeable.
\fBNDI_DEBUG(flags, args)\fP emits a \fBcmn_err()\fP call when \fBNDI_DEVI_DEBUG\fP
is set, with \fBargs\fP supplied as a parenthesized argument list.
.Ss Unavailable declarations
\fBndi_dc_getpath()\fP and \fBndi_dc_getminorname()\fP are declared but are not
implemented in illumos.
\fBndi_event_dump_hdl()\fP is an internal debugging entry point declared only
under \fBDEBUG\fP.
.Sh CONTEXT
Most configuration, device-control, and resource-management functions
may sleep and require user or kernel context.
Only functions that explicitly permit interrupt context should be called
from an interrupt handler.
See each function's CONTEXT section.
.Sh SEE ALSO
